.javascript-electronjs-rule-electron_experimental_features","id":"gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-electronjs-rule-electron_experimental_features","name":"gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-electronjs-rule-electron_experimental_features","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-272","OWASP-A05:2021-Security Misconfiguration","OWASP-A6:2017-Security Misconfiguration","security"],"security-severity":"CRITICAL"},"shortDescription":{"text":"Least privilege violation"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Node integration exposes node.js APIs to the electron app and this can introduce remote code execution vulnerabilities to the application if the app is vulnerable to Cross Site Scripting (XSS).\n"},"help":{"markdown":"Node integration exposes node.js APIs to the electron app and this can introduce remote code execution vulnerabilities to the application if the app is vulnerable to Cross Site Scripting (XSS).\n\n\n#### 💎 Enable cross-file analysis and Pro rules for free at <a href='https://sg.run/pro'>sg.run/pro</a>\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [Semgrep Rule](https://semgrep.dev/r/gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-electronjs-rule-electron_nodejs_integration)\n","text":"Node integration exposes node.js APIs to the electron app and this can introduce remote code execution vulnerabilities to the application if the app is vulnerable to Cross Site Scripting (XSS).\n\n💎 Enable cross-file analysis and Pro rules for free at sg.run/pro"},"helpUri":"https://semgrep.dev/r/gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-electronjs-rule-electron_nodejs_integration","id":"gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-electronjs-rule-electron_nodejs_integration","name":"gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-electronjs-rule-electron_nodejs_integration","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-272","OWASP-A05:2021-Security Misconfiguration","OWASP-A6:2017-Security Misconfiguration","security"],"security-severity":"MEDIUM"},"shortDescription":{"text":"Least privilege violation"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"error"},"fullDescription":{"text":"User controlled data in eval() or similar functions may result in Server Side Injection or Remote Code Injection\n"},"help":{"markdown":"User controlled data in eval() or similar functions may result in Server Side Injection or Remote Code Injection\n\n\n#### 💎 Enable cross-file analysis and Pro rules for free at <a href='https://sg.run/pro'>sg.run/pro</a>\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [Semgrep Rule](https://semgrep.dev/r/gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-eval-rule-eval_nodejs)\n","text":"User controlled data in eval() or similar functions may result in Server Side Injection or Remote Code Injection\n\n💎 Enable cross-file analysis and Pro rules for free at sg.run/pro"},"helpUri":"https://semgrep.dev/r/gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-eval-rule-eval_nodejs","id":"gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-eval-rule-eval_nodejs","name":"gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-eval-rule-eval_nodejs","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-95","OWASP-A03:2021-Injection","OWASP-A1:2017-Injection","security"],"security-severity":"HIGH"},"shortDescription":{"text":"Improper neutralization of directives in dynamically evaluated code ('Eval Injection')"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"error"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Passing untrusted user input directly into the require() function without proper \nvalidation or sanitization can possibly cause a vulnerability known as remote code execution (RCE). \nAn attacker could manipulate the input to load and execute arbitrary code from external sources, \npotentially leading to severe security breaches such as data theft, system compromise, \nor unauthorized access.\nTo mitigate this risk, it's crucial to validate and sanitize user input\nthoroughly before passing it to functions like require(), ensuring that only trusted and safe inputs are utilized.\n\nFollowing is an example of secure validation against allowlist to prevent the vulnerability:\n```\n// Define a list of explicitly allowed packages for require\nconst allowedPkgs = [\n    'package1',\n    'package2',\n    'package3'\n];\n\napp.get(\"/eval/require/7\", async (req, res) => {\n    var isAllowed = allowedPkgs.includes(req.query.name);  \n    if (isAllowed) {\n        // ok: rules_lgpl_javascript_eval_rule-eval-require\n        var cp = require(req.query.name);\n        cp.exec('ls', (error, stdout, stderr) => {\n            console.log(\"exec output : \\n\", stdout)\n        });        \n    }\n    res.send(\"Please check console logs.\");\n});\n```\n"},"help":{"markdown":"Passing untrusted user input directly into the require() function without proper \nvalidation or sanitization can possibly cause a vulnerability known as remote code execution (RCE). \nAn attacker could manipulate the input to load and execute arbitrary code from external sources, \npotentially leading to severe security breaches such as data theft, system compromise, \nor unauthorized access.\nTo mitigate this risk, it's crucial to validate and sanitize user input\nthoroughly before passing it to functions like require(), ensuring that only trusted and safe inputs are utilized.\n\nFollowing is an example of secure validation against allowlist to prevent the vulnerability:\n```\n// Define a list of explicitly allowed packages for require\nconst allowedPkgs = [\n    'package1',\n    'package2',\n    'package3'\n];\n\napp.get(\"/eval/require/7\", async (req, res) => {\n    var isAllowed = allowedPkgs.includes(req.query.name);  \n    if (isAllowed) {\n        // ok: rules_lgpl_javascript_eval_rule-eval-require\n        var cp = require(req.query.name);\n        cp.exec('ls', (error, stdout, stderr) => {\n            console.log(\"exec output : \\n\", stdout)\n        });        \n    }\n    res.send(\"Please check console logs.\");\n});\n```\n\n\n#### 💎 Enable cross-file analysis and Pro rules for free at <a href='https://sg.run/pro'>sg.run/pro</a>\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [Semgrep Rule](https://semgrep.dev/r/gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-eval-rule-eval_require)\n","text":"Passing untrusted user input directly into the require() function without proper \nvalidation or sanitization can possibly cause a vulnerability known as remote code execution (RCE). \nAn attacker could manipulate the input to load and execute arbitrary code from external sources, \npotentially leading to severe security breaches such as data theft, system compromise, \nor unauthorized access.\nTo mitigate this risk, it's crucial to validate and sanitize user input\nthoroughly before passing it to functions like require(), ensuring that only trusted and safe inputs are utilized.\n\nFollowing is an example of secure validation against allowlist to prevent the vulnerability:\n```\n// Define a list of explicitly allowed packages for require\nconst allowedPkgs = [\n    'package1',\n    'package2',\n    'package3'\n];\n\napp.get(\"/eval/require/7\", async (req, res) => {\n    var isAllowed = allowedPkgs.includes(req.query.name);  \n    if (isAllowed) {\n        // ok: rules_lgpl_javascript_eval_rule-eval-require\n        var cp = require(req.query.name);\n        cp.exec('ls', (error, stdout, stderr) => {\n            console.log(\"exec output : \\n\", stdout)\n        });        \n    }\n    res.send(\"Please check console logs.\");\n});\n```\n\n💎 Enable cross-file analysis and Pro rules for free at sg.run/pro"},"helpUri":"https://semgrep.dev/r/gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-eval-rule-eval_require","id":"gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-eval-rule-eval_require","name":"gitlab.nodejs_scan.javascript-eval-rule-eval_require","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-706","OWASP-A03:2021-Injection","OWASP-A1:2017-Injection","security"],"security-severity":"CRITICAL"},"shortDescription":{"text":"Use of incorrectly-resolved name or reference"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"error"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Found an insecure gRPC connection. This creates a connection without encryption to a gRPC client/server. A malici